Leave these:

	/etc/fonts/conf.d/10-autohint.conf
	/etc/fonts/conf.d/10-scale-bitmap-fonts.conf

Remove the slight hinting:

	/etc/fonts/conf.d/10-hinting-slight.conf
	
Remove these, as they prevent terminus otb to be used and mess others:

	/etc/fonts/conf.d/70-no-bitmaps-except-emoji.conf
	/etc/fonts/conf.d/48-guessfamily.conf
	/etc/fonts/conf.d/49-sansserif.conf

You may wish to add this to /etc/pkgadd.conf (so files are not installed
upon fontconfig update):

	INSTALL		^etc/fonts/conf\.d/10-hinting-slight\.conf		NO
	INSTALL		^etc/fonts/conf\.d/70-no-bitmaps-except-emoji\.conf	NO
	INSTALL		^etc/fonts/conf\.d/48-guessfamily\.conf			NO
	INSTALL		^etc/fonts/conf\.d/49-sansserif\.conf			NO

This sets freetype by /etc/profile.d/fonts-conf.sh, as:

	export FREETYPE_PROPERTIES="truetype:interpreter-version=35 cff:no-stem-darkening=0 autofitter:no-stem-darkening=0

Another option is simply:

	export FREETYPE_PROPERTIES="truetype:interpreter-version=35"

NOTE:
truetype:interpreter-version=35 makes TrueType fonts (most .ttf files,
including the Microsoft fonts) use FreeType's older hinting engine. Text
looks sharper and crisper, closer to Windows XP/7. FreeType’s current
default (version 40) gives softer, slightly blurrier but smoother text.

cff:no-stem-darkening=0 switches on "stem darkening" for CFF fonts,
the PostScript-style .otf fonts such as Apple SF fonts. It makes thin
strokes a little heavier, so small text looks less spindly. The double
negative (no-...=0) is FreeType's naming: "no darkening" = false, so
darkening is on.

autofitter:no-stem-darkening=0 does the same for fonts drawn with
FreeType's autohinter. The fonts.conf turns autohinting on for many 
fonts, so this affects all of those.
